comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T02:20:46.064Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 on PR #1762 (Codex gpt-6.2-sol xhigh, 13 findings; Greptile, 3 findings that overlap Codex). Every finding was accepted and fixed in the second commit.\n\nBridge integrity: the bridge envelope gains segment_digest, a digest under the record's algorithm of every record from the first break up to the bridge. Previously only the break entries were named, so an interior record with its seal stripped and its patch changed still verified whenever a later patch overwrote the changed value. Inside a segment, any entry whose recorded before-state replay proves (for example after a recorded change overwrote the edited field) must also match its recorded after-state. New failure code: bridge_segment_mismatch.\n\nDissolution: a union merge, a rewrite before the segment, or history-redact must re-anchor a bridged segment on replay that lacks the unrecorded change. Each now marks the covered entries with the sealed field replay_inexact and keeps the original bridge as rewrite evidence. Redaction used to refuse every bridged stream with bridge_without_break; it now dissolves the bridge and verifies. replayHistoryToTarget (restore and get --at) refuses such versions with the new code history_version_unreconstructable, and refuses versions inside a verified open segment the same way (previously history_replay_invalid).\n\nRepair: the bridge anchors on the epoch the verified prefix proves instead of the rewrite resolver's epoch-1 fallback for implicit streams. Reconciliation reuses the tail's epoch-2 variant. The preview reports reconciliation and the history_repair_reconcile_discards_events warning, like the rewriting repair. The append refuses with the new reason stream_changed when the bytes read for the write differ from the verified snapshot.\n\nMerge: equivalent_bridges now requires both sides and the result to verify, and appends to ours' raw bytes (CRLF-safe). Salvage parses trimmed lines like the shared reader (BOM).\n\nPackage: the import-closure check moved from package-artifact-gate into package-distribution's leased staging. It runs on the exact staged bytes and lexes with TypeScript preProcessFile, so comments and template text are not edges and from /* comment */ \"x\" is. A negative control without the graph.js exclusion refuses the pack.\n\nRegression tests: tests/integration/lifecycle/history-bridge.integration.spec.ts (17 tests), tests/unit/sdk/history/append-only-repair.spec.ts (epoch-2 implicit prefix, field-frozen variant, stream_changed and UTF-8 guards), tests/unit/scripts/package-distribution.spec.ts (lexer controls plus a real refused pack).\n"
  "2026-11-21T03:05:20.381Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 2 on PR #1762: Codex (9 findings) and CodeRabbit (5 findings on the round-1 head). All were accepted and fixed in the third commit.\n\nRoot cause shared by most of them: knowledge that a bridged segment's versions cannot be reconstructed was only computed from a fully verified stream at rewrite time. Rewrites that reseal, interleave or repair records first could therefore dissolve a bridge without marking its versions. Fixed at one point: locateBridgedSegments (src/core/history/bridge.ts) finds each bridge's segment from its own commitments, its first named break plus its segment digest, without needing the stream before it to verify. Markers only restrict reads, so a forged bridge cannot widen what verifies. materializeBridgedSegments writes replay_inexact onto covered records before a rewrite can void the bridge. Users: provenance normalization (before resealing), merge unions (records a bridge covers on either original branch, matched by union identity, before interleaving), reanchor (a bridge dissolved because an earlier record was repaired) and redaction. history-redact now refuses a stream whose bridge does not locate its segment (history_redact_bridge_unlocated) instead of dropping that bridge's evidence. A compaction checkpoint at an inexact version inherits the marker.\n\nVerifier: every record from the break to the bridge must be complete (record_incomplete); link-only checks are not enough for a record without a patch array. A declared epoch inside a segment, including on the break entry, now governs the unversioned records after it.\n\nRepair: the record anchors on the newest declared epoch, otherwise the epoch the verified prefix proves. Its epoch-2 variant must be proven by replay or by the on-disk item, else it refuses with hash_surface_ambiguous. The write uses O_APPEND on the handle whose bytes were checked, syncs, and refuses stream_changed when the path no longer names that inode, so it never replaces bytes another writer added. The UTF-8 check also runs before planning, so dry-run and apply agree.\n\nMerge identity now includes a record's bridge, so differing bridges never count as one shared record (an invalid bridge on one side can no longer make the merge identical). health keeps --append-only in the bulk remedy for history_drift_chain_mismatch. Import closure now uses a TypeScript AST walk (regex-literal control). TypeScript resolves from the packed checkout first and then from the script, so release controls copied without node_modules still pack.\n\nCoverage: 212 related spec files (4,361 tests) pass with no uncovered changed line in the touched modules. The first hosted coverage run had found 4 gaps (since fixed), including item-revision.ts line 361: the decoder now refuses a null line before that catch, so its spec now uses a record without a patch array to keep exercising the catch.\n"
  "2026-11-21T03:32:57.523Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 3 on PR #1762 (Codex, 7 findings) plus two hosted CI failures on the round-1 head. All were accepted and fixed in the fourth commit.\n\nReplay mutated recorded patches: normalizeReplayPatchOps shared op values with the replay document, so a later op of the same patch changed the recorded value before the segment digest was computed. It now deep-copies values, so an unsealed record's original value can no longer be changed under a sealed bridge.\n\nUnlocatable bridges: locateBridgedSegments now finds a segment by its first break plus digest, then by that break's recorded anchors (which survive the resealing that marking applies), and otherwise yields the conservative range from the record after the previous bridge. A rewrite therefore never serves an unlocatable bridge's versions as exact, at the cost of over-restricting.\n\nMerges: every merge path, including identical and fast-forward results and equivalent bridges, keeps a replay_inexact marker that either branch already carries. Only the union path, which dissolves bridges, also marks each branch's bridge coverage, matched by union identity. Paths that keep live bridges never mark their segments (that would void the digests). A projected reanchor leaves marking to the merge driver.\n\nEpochs: for an implicit stream, the appended record keeps the epoch the verified prefix proves unless the tail's own anchor proves only other epochs; it then takes the newest of those, and refuses hash_surface_ambiguous when nothing proves one. The verifier requires the bridge record to keep the epoch the stream declared (bridge_epoch_mismatch). Covered records must carry anchors that are well-formed digests of their algorithm (record_incomplete).\n\nConcurrent same-inode writer: after the O_APPEND write, the repair re-reads the handle and reports stream_changed when the bytes are not exactly the verified prefix plus its record. It never rolls back peer bytes.\n\nPackaging: hosted CI failed registry-acceptance-workflow, because the preserved release controls run in a foreign workspace without node_modules and the TypeScript lexer could not load. The import-closure check is now dependency-free. Under the build lease, listExcludedModules lists the modules the checkout holds under shipped module directories that the files list leaves out, and findExcludedModuleReferences refuses any staged module containing a quoted relative path that resolves to one of them. Every spelling counts (static, dynamic, require, worker URLs), and it fails closed for comments that name an excluded path.\n"
  "2026-11-21T04:18:29.084Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 4 on PR #1762: Codex with gpt-6.2-astra after gpt-6.2-sol twice stopped at a content filter. 16 findings: 13 fixed, 3 deferred to pm-vtg2d8.\n\nFixed:\n- Location grades. locateBridgedSegments marks a segment exact only when its digest matches from a start that the first break's record hash or anchors name; the record before the break may be damaged. A break-anchor start is otherwise accepted only when every covered record already carries replay_inexact (a rewrite marked it from an exact location). Anything else is unproven: it marks the conservative range since the previous bridge plus the bridge record itself. A later matching anchor pair can no longer shorten a segment.\n- history-redact dissolves only exact segments and refuses otherwise. It rewrites the live item from replay, so a damaged segment must not authorize that.\n- reanchorHistoryEntries derives coverage itself for projected rewrites too. Only a caller that declares callerMarksBridgeCoverage, as the merge driver does, skips it.\n- Unions mark by merged position: every event placed between a branch segment's first covered record and its bridge.\n- A neutralized bridge matches its original record's identity on later merges, so there are no duplicate repair records.\n- Identical, fast-forward and equivalent results never mark records that a retained exact live bridge covers.\n- An effect-free patch (empty or test-only) cannot be bridged with differing anchors.\n- A repair that only dissolves a stray bridge or adds markers is persisted (ReanchorResult.entriesResealed).\n- An unsupported tail digest is a typed refusal.\n- The checked append opens with O_RDWR|O_APPEND and never creates the path; ENOENT becomes stream_changed.\n- The closure check resolves extensionless, directory-index and query or fragment specifiers.\n- The docs no longer claim a rewriting repair keeps a bridged segment byte-identical: it keeps the records, while only --append-only keeps the bytes.\n\nDeferred to pm-vtg2d8, none of which loses data or serves a restore: before-values shown by history diffs, epoch-2 variant selection with duplicate replay matches, and holding the closer to a proven variant.\n"
  "2026-11-21T04:50:44.152Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 5 on PR #1762. Two full-diff Codex runs (gpt-6.2-astra) stopped at a content filter. A narrower gpt-6.2-sol review of the latest fix commits found 4 regressions they introduced, all fixed in the next commit. (1) Merge marking keyed merged positions by identity, so repeated identical events (same author, timestamp and patch) marked only the last copy. It now maps the k-th occurrence on a branch to the k-th occurrence in the merged stream. (2) Neutral bridge copies aliased to their original's identity created opposite ordering edges when two reciprocal equivalent-bridge merges were merged again. The equivalent-bridges path now accepts any suffixes made only of the same repair, live or neutral: ours is kept and only theirs' repairs ours does not represent are appended, so no ordering is needed. (3) entriesResealed counted epoch-label-only changes, so a clean mixed-era stream was rewritten by a rewriting repair. It now counts only bridge dissolution and new replay_inexact markers. (4) The package check stripped \"#\" before judging a specifier, while require() reads it literally; both spellings are now checked. Remaining finding classes are tracked on pm-vtg2d8.\n"
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T01:25:25.503Z","harness:claude-code","Design as implemented, 2026-11-21. One sealed history_repair record carries bridge {version: 1, breaks: [{gap_from, gap_to, record_hash}]}. gap_from is the after_hash recorded before the break, gap_to the before_hash recorded after it, and record_hash belongs to the break entry (null for a legacy entry). Its patch is jsonPatch.compare(replay without the unrecorded change, on-disk item). Its before_hash is the last recorded after_hash, and its after_hash is the on-disk item hash, so replay re-anchors on the truth at the bridge.\n\nVerifier (verifyHistoryChainWithVersion, src/core/history/replay.ts): an anchored before-hash mismatch opens a segment only when the next bridge's first descriptor names exactly that break. Inside the segment each patch must still apply strictly, and each recorded link must hold or be the next named break. At the bridge, every named break must have occurred, its before_hash must link, and replay plus its patch must hash to its after_hash. Failures: bridge_invalid (malformed, unsealed or non-history_repair, pre-scanned so it wins over an earlier break), bridge_unnamed_break, bridge_names_absent_break, bridge_link_mismatch, bridge_after_hash_mismatch, bridge_without_break. The result adds bridged_segments. DRIFT_CACHE_VERSION 11 -> 12 so an older pm never trusts a verdict computed under these semantics.\n\nRepair (src/sdk/history/append-only-repair.ts): name the first replay mismatch plus every later recorded-link mismatch, build the record, and require the extended stream to verify before writing. The write appends to the exact bytes read under the lock and refuses non-UTF-8 streams. Refusal: history_append_only_repair_unavailable with reason chain_failure_not_a_break, patch_does_not_replay, item_missing, stream_not_utf8 or bridge_does_not_verify; next step is the rewriting repair. The pm health remedy for history_drift_chain_mismatch now names --append-only. --all passes through.\n\nRewrite interaction: reanchorHistoryEntries keeps a verified bridged segment byte-identical while nothing before it changed, so the rewriting repair is a no-op on a bridged stream. A rewrite before the segment, or any projected rewrite (merge union), dissolves the bridge into an ordinary record and keeps the original sealed record as rewrite evidence (retainPriorRecord), so a stale bridge never survives. Merge: two clones that bridged the same break (identical bridge, patch, anchors, version and algorithm) merge as equivalent_bridges. Ours stays byte-for-byte and theirs is appended as a neutral record with an empty patch and the original in evidence, so both repairs keep their provenance.\n\nKnown limit: versions between a break and its bridge lack the unrecorded change, so restore and get --at refuse them with history_replay_invalid. Versions before the break and from the bridge onward stay addressable. Cross-version: an older pm reports drift on a bridged stream, and its rewriting repair keeps the unknown bridge field. This pm then reports bridge_without_break, and its own rewriting repair dissolves the bridge."
